<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The languages this app is actually translated into, and nothing else.

  From API 33 this file is what Android's own Settings → Apps → plMail →
  Language offers, and it is taken at its word: a locale listed here with no
  values- directory behind it gives the user a language they can select and
  which then renders in English, silently, because resource resolution falls
  back rather than failing. So the list is exactly values/ and values-de/, and
  LocalesConfigTest asserts that against the source tree so the two cannot drift
  apart when a translation is added or dropped.

  "en" rather than a region, because values/ is the unqualified default: English
  here is the base resources rather than any particular English.

  Written by hand rather than generated. AGP can produce this file from the
  resource directories it finds (androidResources.generateLocaleConfig), and the
  two cannot both exist, because declaring android:localeConfig while generation
  is on is a build error. It is written out because :app takes translations from
  library modules as well as its own, and a generated list is a list nobody
  reviews before it reaches a picker the user sees.

  Note the prose here avoids a double hyphen. XML forbids one inside a comment,
  and AAPT fails the resource compile on it rather than warning.
-->
<locale-config xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <locale android:name="en" />
    <locale android:name="de" />
</locale-config>
